{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"learned-vapory-prawn","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Class\n\nThree related POSIX-process assumptions, all in job `100874330553`:\n\n1. **3 × `OSError: [WinError 193] %1 is not a valid Win32 application`** from\n   `_winapi.CreateProcess`, in `tests/unit/test_child_oracle_fail_open.py`\n   (`test_the_suicide_shim_really_produces_a_signal_killed_child`,\n   `test_reconcile_routing_oracle_rejects_a_signal_killed_child`,\n   `test_pandoc_colour_oracle_rejects_a_signal_killed_child`). Windows has no shebang handling: a\n   `#!`-headed script handed straight to `CreateProcess` is not an executable image.\n2. **Negative returncodes do not exist.** The log carries\n   `AssertionError: a signal kill through this site's spawn path surfaced as a POSITIVE returncode\n   (1)`. On POSIX a signal-killed child reports `-N`; rebar's child oracles use that sign to\n   distinguish \"killed\" from \"exited non-zero\". Windows has no signals in that sense, so the\n   discriminator is gone and the oracle's *logic*, not just its test, needs a platform-appropriate\n   substitute.\n3. **`.sh`-dependent gate tests**: `re-omitting infra/scripts/materialize-mcp-upstream.sh did not\n   return the gate to RED`, `…compose-up.sh…`, `…infra/gerrit/materialize-deploy-key.sh…` — gates\n   that shell out to bash scripts that do not run on a bare Windows runner.\n\n## Note on the bug's \".EXE console-script invocation\" class\n\nThe bug's class list named `.EXE console-script invocation` as an independent class. The log shows\nthose failures are\n`subprocess.CalledProcessError: Command '['D:\\a\\rebar\\rebar\\.venv\\Scripts\\rebar.EXE', 'create',\n'task', …]'` whose captured stderr is the S1 `flock` `AttributeError`. **They are downstream of S1,\nnot a separate class**, and are expected to clear when S1 lands. This story covers the genuine\nexec-model problems above instead. Re-measure after S1 and fold in any residual `.EXE` failures that\nsurvive.\n\n## What the fix must decide\n\n- Spawn shebang scripts through their interpreter explicitly (`[sys.executable, script]`,\n  `[\"bash\", script]`) rather than relying on the kernel's shebang handling.\n- Give the child oracles a platform-defined \"killed\" predicate instead of `returncode < 0`.\n- Decide whether the `.sh`-dependent infra gates are **in scope** for Windows at all. It is entirely\n  defensible that they are not — a Windows developer runs the test suite, not the deploy scripts —\n  but if so, mark them `skipif` with the reason cited and record the scope carve-out in the ADR's\n  consequences rather than leaving them silently red.\n\n## Blocked by S1\n\n## Proving command\n\n```\nuv run --no-sync pytest tests/unit/test_child_oracle_fail_open.py -p no:cacheprovider -q\n```\non `windows-latest`, plus:\n```\nuv run --no-sync pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -q -p no:cacheprovider 2>&1 \\\n  | grep -cE \"WinError 193|POSITIVE returncode\"   # must be 0\n```\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Zero `WinError 193` and zero POSITIVE-returncode assertion failures in a Windows sweep.\n2. The child-oracle \"was this child killed?\" predicate is platform-defined and tested on both.\n3. Any `.sh`-dependent gate excluded from Windows is explicitly skipped with a cited reason, and the\n   carve-out appears in the ADR's consequences.\n","id":"7daf-59a6-5f9a-4698","parent_id":"55da-cee1-e556-4735","priority":3,"tags":["windows","portability","subprocess","signals"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"S9: POSIX exec and signal semantics — shebang scripts are not Win32 apps (WinError 193) and there are no negative returncodes"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788571434968472963,"uuid":"a7532738-fc1f-4bfc-b2a6-d39ea56e2b5e"}